.TH GITTREE 1 "2026-10-01" "gittree @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gittree \- the history of the git refs that you tick
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gittree
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.RI [ REF ...]
.RB [ \-S
.I TEXT
|
.B \-G
.IR REGEX ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-\-
.IR PATH ...]
.br
.B git tree
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.RI [ REF ...]
.RB [ \-S
.I TEXT
|
.B \-G
.IR REGEX ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-\-
.IR PATH ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gittree
shows the history of a git repository in a window, as
.BR gitg (1)
does, with a checkbox for each branch, remote branch and tag. Only the
commits that a ticked ref reaches are drawn, and only the ticked refs are
labelled. A commit that a ticked branch shares with an unticked one still
shows, as part of the ticked branch.
.PP
The refs are on the left, in three groups: branches, remote branches and
tags. A name with slashes is divided into groups at each slash. The box of
a group ticks or unticks every ref in it, and also opens or folds the group.
.PP
A click on a commit, or Enter, shows its details and its diff below the
refs and the list. A click on the same commit, Enter or Escape hides them
again. A double-click is two clicks: it shows them and hides them again. A
click that opens a file, or a click on Expand all, fills the window with
the diff. A click that closes a file does not change the window. When the
commit changes only one file, Enter fills the window at once. Escape, the back arrow or the close button
at the top right of the diff brings back the refs and the list.
.PP
.B gittree
is read only. It writes nothing to the repository: no ref is moved, no
index is written, and no working tree is touched.
.PP
With no
.I REF
and no tick option, gittree ticks the refs that you last chose in that
repository. This works from any folder in the repository. A local branch
that is new since then is ticked too, as it is in an open window. A new
remote branch or a new tag is not. Until you change a tick, every ref is
ticked. A run with a
.I REF
or a tick option ticks those refs, and gittree keeps none of the ticks
that you change in that run. gittree keeps the ticks in its own settings,
not in the repository. Outside a repository, the window opens a list of
the repositories that you opened before.
.PP
.B git tree
runs the same program, because the package installs
.B git\-tree
as a second name for it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I REF
Tick only these refs. A glob such as
.B 'feature/*'
matches the short or the full names.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Tick every ref.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-local
Tick every local branch.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-remotes
Tick every remote branch.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tags
Tick every tag.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-text " \fITEXT\fR"
Draw only the commits that add or remove
.IR TEXT .
A commit adds or removes the text when the number of times it appears in
a file the commit changes goes up or down, as in
.BR "git log \-S" .
A merge never does. The graph joins across the commits that it leaves out.
The search runs in the background. The window opens with the search bar
on Changed lines, which shows the text and can change it later (see
.BR SEARCH ).
The bar ignores case unless its Match case switch is on.
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\-regex " \fIREGEX\fR"
Draw only the commits whose added or removed lines match
.IR REGEX ,
a POSIX extended regular expression, as in
.BR "git log \-G" .
A commit that moves a line, or edits a line that still matches, is drawn
too. A merge never is. The Regex switch of Changed lines does the same in
the window.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
With
.B \-S
or
.BR \-G ,
ignore case.
.TP
.BI "\-\- " PATH
Draw only the commits that change these files or folders. With one file,
the history follows it through its earlier names, as
.B git log \-\-follow
does, and shows no merges. The window opens with the paths in Files of the
search bar, which can change them. It splits paths at spaces, as the shell does, and takes globs such
as
.BR '*.yaml' .
The diff of a commit that matches shows only those files. A right click on
a file in the diff offers Show history of this file, which puts that file
in Files.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wd
Open the list of repositories, not the repository of the current folder.
The desktop entry uses it. It takes no ref, no path, no text, no regex and
no tick option.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help and exit.
.PP
Options add up:
.B gittree \-l origin/master
ticks every local branch and
.BR origin/master .
.SH SEARCH
When the pane below the list is shut, Ctrl+F opens the search bar of the
list. The search button in the header bar opens it at any time. The
choice on the left of its
field picks what the field searches: Messages, authors and hashes, Changed
lines, or Files. Each choice keeps its own text, Match case and Regex, and
their searches add up: a commit matches when it meets every choice that
holds a text. The matches are marked in the list, and Display matches only
hides the commits that do not match. A regex is a POSIX extended regular
expression, as in
.BR "git log \-G" .
Changed lines and Files search when Enter is pressed; Messages, authors
and hashes searches as you type. It looks in the subject, the message, the
author and the hash of each commit in the list. Words narrow it further:
.BI author: TEXT
looks in the name and the email of the author,
.BI message: TEXT
in the subject and the message,
.BI hash: TEXT
in the hash, and
.BI before: DATE
and
.BI after: DATE
keep the commits whose author date is before, or on or after, the start of
a year, a month or a day, given as YYYY, YYYY\-MM or YYYY\-MM\-DD in local
time. A value with spaces goes in double quotes. Every part must hold.
When the bar closes, every search in it is lifted. The bar keeps its texts
and switches, so when it opens again its text is selected, and Enter
searches again. While a search of the changed lines or the files applies,
the yellow bar above the list says so. Its close button lifts every
search and empties the fields. When
no commit of the ticked refs matches a messages search, the bar counts the
matches on the other refs, and Tick and show ticks a ref that holds the newest of them and
selects it: the branch of HEAD, else the first local branch by name, then
remote branch, then tag.
.PP
The find bar of the diff has Match case and Regex too, and keeps its text
when it closes.
.SH THE MENU OF A COMMIT
A right click on a commit in the list opens its menu. Copy hash copies its
hash, and Copy name, on a label, the name of that ref. Branches and tags
with this commit lists every ref whose history holds the commit, and a
choice ticks or unticks it. First tag with this commit names the tag that
.B git describe \-\-contains
gives, and a choice selects it. Merged into names the merge that brought
the commit into the branch of HEAD, or says that the commit was made on
that branch.
.PP
A right click on a ref in the panel offers Copy name and Go to where it
splits from, with the other ticked refs. A choice selects the newest
commit that the two refs share, as
.B git merge-base
finds it.
.SH THE HISTORY OF LINES
A right click on a line of the diff, or on lines that you selected, offers
Show history of this line or Show history of the selected lines. The list
then shows only the commits that changed those lines, as
.B git log \-L
does, from the commit on show. Removed lines are read from the parent that
the diff compares with. The close button of the yellow bar brings back the
list as it was.
.PP
On a line that the commit did not add, the same menu offers Go to the
commit that last changed this line, as
.B git blame
finds it. When Display matches only hides that commit, a bar says so and
offers Show every commit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR Click ", " Enter
Show or hide the details and the diff of a commit. When the commit changes
only one file, Enter fills the window with the diff.
.TP
.BR "Open a file" ", " "Expand all"
Fill the window with the diff. A click that closes a file does not.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F
While the pane is open, open or close the find bar of the diff. While it
is shut, open or close the search bar. With text selected on one line of
the diff, search the diff for that text.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+F
Open the search bar on Changed lines, or close it when it is open there.
With text selected on one line of the diff, put that text in the field.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " Ctrl+G
Go to the next commit that matches. In the find bar of the diff, go to the
next match, and open its file.
.TP
.BR Shift+Enter ", " Ctrl+Shift+G
Go to the commit before that matches. In the find bar of the diff, go to
the match before.
.TP
.B Escape
Close one thing, from the bottom of the window up: the find bar of the
diff, then the full diff, then the details of the commit, then the search
bar, which lifts every search in it. A field that has the focus does not
change this order.
.TP
.B F5
Read the repository again.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
Quit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal run.
.TP
.B 1
A ref matches nothing, a ref, a path, a text or a tick option is given
outside a repository, or the display cannot be opened.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git\-log (1),
.BR gitg (1)
.SH AUTHOR
alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>
.PP
gittree is built from a vendored subtree of gitg, which is copyright its own
authors and licensed GPL-2.0-or-later.
